{"kind":"artifact","text":"Two closed-environment claims carry their qualification where they are made. Exact clean pushed head b2cee3752d120f5c6b292b286fb899a6d21c49f2 on request/repo-remote-link; tree f0d7072c, checkout clean, origin matching. Base: merge-base with main is c679fb73; main is 3a5d952c, both verified at filing. Under planner's recovery child ddaf16c9 and my promise 0e00bcd7. This supersedes ef88847c at the same head, which described a settled ruling as an intention.\n\nTHE THREAT MODEL IS SETTLED AND THIS ARTIFACT SAYS SO. Hugh ruled at 57df8d25 that the operator's ambient Git environment — the routing family, the GIT_CONFIG family, HOME and XDG_CONFIG_HOME — is outside gitseq's threat model. gitseq trusts the environment of the operator who runs it, and defending a hostile operator-controlled environment is a non-goal. That is a ruling, not a direction of travel.\n\nMY PREVIOUS ARTIFACT GOT THIS WRONG BY ONE RECORD. It said hugh \"intends\" the environment to be out of scope and that a clarified ruling \"may\" reframe the claim. The ruling was already effective when I filed — it landed one sequence earlier. I wrote the sentence from the state of the world when I drafted the text rather than when I signed it, which is the same defect as the provenance line this very round exists to fix, applied to a fact I had been tracking all night.\n\nWHAT THE CODE STILL DOES, AND WHY THAT IS NOT A THREAT CLAIM. These five reads run under a stated child environment on Unix. That is a determinism property: the answers depend on the repository the resident was pointed at rather than on whatever the invoking shell happened to carry, so the same repository gives the same answer to the browser regardless of who started the process. It is not a defence against a hostile operator, and under hugh's ruling it should not be described as one. The two are different claims about the same mechanism and only the first is one this project makes.\n\nWHAT THE CHANGE IN THIS HEAD IS. gitStatedEnvironment at app.go:214 and the closed-world test at repo_remote_test.go:711 both opened absolutely and narrowed to Unix several paragraphs later; standing alone each was false, because Go's os/exec adds SYSTEMROOT to what a Windows child receives. Both now carry the qualification in the opening sentence. Line numbers re-derived at this head. Tree byte-identical to the withdrawn ebc0502a, so only provenance differs.\n\nWHY THE COMMIT WAS REMADE. ebc0502a's canonical Rests-On named the retired child 1c12a6d3. I had predicted that resting on that row would poison the durable records, asked planner to re-anchor, then built the commit against it while waiting. A trailer cannot be corrected after the fact the way a record can. Codex declined review on that ground and was right.\n\nTHE SWEEP. The rest of internal/app and its tests carry no other absolute closed-environment claim.\n\nNO BEHAVIOUR CHANGES. The construction, the regressions, their hard controls and the citation exit-code fail-closed rule are exactly as reviewed at 5a4ee9e9.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached. npm test 131 pass, 0 fail. gofmt -l . empty; go build, go vet, GOOS=windows go build ./internal/app/ and git diff --check all exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty; make ui-check exit 0. Not run by me: go test -race, and any gate on Linux.","body":{"commit":"b2cee3752d120f5c6b292b286fb899a6d21c49f2","dead_basis_override":"true","path":"internal/app"}}